sec.p:
Figure 2c–h compares the screw-dislocation velocities with and without SRO at different temperatures. At 300 K (Fig. 2c), the influence of SRO is clearly apparent; based on the shift in the velocity curve, we can estimate the extra strengthening from SRO to be ~180 MPa. We can attribute this SRO effect to be associated with the break-up of local chemical order which creates diffuse antiphase boundaries (DAPBs)48. When the screw-dislocation glides in a system containing SRO, the order will be partially destroyed and a DAPB will be created along the glide plane, with an energy cost per area γDAPB. A classical estimation of this strengthening effect of SRO by Fisher48 can be written as: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
