The influence of SRO on the edge dislocation motion is investigated by comparing the velocity data in Fig. 5a and b. For both low and intermediate stress regimes, the edge dislocation velocities for the system with SRO differ significantly from those in the RSS sample. In the low-stress regime, the glide of an edge dislocation in the RSS sample shows the “friction stress” effect, where the dislocation stays immobile until the applied shear stress exceeds a critical value; this critical stress decreases as the temperature increases. A similar phenomenon has been observed in other alloy systems47,54,55, which can be attributed to the pinning of the dislocation due to the ﻿collective interaction of random solute atoms. However, in Fig. 5b, with the presence of SRO, the friction phenomenon is not as effective as in the RSS sample. Instead, the edge dislocation is not immobile but glides at a very low average velocity (<2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
